          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>A patient is referred to a surgeon for suspected parathyroid disease. Which of t</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>A patient is referred to a surgeon for suspected parathyroid disease. Which of the following is the most likely initial clinical finding?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>A palpable midline neck swelling</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Parathyroid glands are only a few millimetres across and sit on the posterior surface of the thyroid. They rarely produce a palpable neck mass, even when diseased.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>An incidentally discovered raised serum calcium</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. Parathyroid disease typically presents as a biochemical abnormality — raised or lowered serum calcium — rather than a physical finding in the neck.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Hoarseness of voice due to nerve compression</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Hoarseness from recurrent laryngeal nerve involvement is more characteristic of thyroid malignancy or a post-surgical complication, not primary parathyroid disease.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>A hard, fixed lump in the posterior triangle of the neck</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>A hard, fixed mass in the posterior triangle suggests lymphadenopathy or a metastatic deposit, not a parathyroid disorder.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "Parathyroid disease does not present as a neck mass. It announces itself through deranged serum calcium and its systemic consequences — stones, bones, abdominal groans, and psychiatric moans."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Well done. Parathyroid disease announces itself as a calcium number, not as a lump in the neck — the classic pattern is 'stones, bones, abdominal groans, and psychiatric moans.'"
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
