          },
          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>A patient with long-standing chronic kidney disease presents with a low serum ca</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>A patient with long-standing chronic kidney disease presents with a low serum calcium and markedly elevated PTH. Which type of hyperparathyroidism does this biochemical pattern indicate?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Primary hyperparathyroidism</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>In primary hyperparathyroidism, calcium is high and PTH is high or inappropriately normal — an autonomous gland drives calcium up. The low calcium here points away from primary disease.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Tertiary hyperparathyroidism</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Tertiary hyperparathyroidism develops after prolonged secondary disease when the glands become autonomous. In that setting, serum calcium is elevated — not low as in this case.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Secondary hyperparathyroidism</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. The combination of low calcium with elevated PTH indicates a compensatory parathyroid response. Chronic kidney disease impairs vitamin D activation and phosphate excretion, driving calcium down. The parathyroids respond appropriately by increasing PTH secretion.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Malignant hypercalcaemia</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>In malignant hypercalcaemia, calcium is high but PTH is suppressed — the hypercalcaemia is driven by tumour-derived factors, not the parathyroid glands. The low calcium here rules this out.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "In secondary hyperparathyroidism, the parathyroids are responding to chronic hypocalcaemia — usually driven by CKD or vitamin D deficiency. Calcium is low and PTH is elevated as a compensatory response, not an autonomous one."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Excellent. Recognising the Ca²⁺–PTH pattern in context is the foundation of every parathyroid diagnosis — secondary hyperparathyroidism is a compensatory response to chronic hypocalcaemia."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
