          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>During a focused parathyroidectomy for a solitary parathyroid adenoma, which int</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>During a focused parathyroidectomy for a solitary parathyroid adenoma, which intraoperative finding confirms that the surgery has been curative?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Visualisation of all four parathyroid glands during exploration</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Bilateral exploration to identify all four glands is performed when localisation is uncertain or four-gland hyperplasia is suspected. In a focused approach, only the target adenoma is removed.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>A fall in intraoperative PTH of more than 50% after excision of the adenoma</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. PTH has a very short half-life. A drop of more than 50% after adenoma removal confirms that no remaining autonomous parathyroid tissue is driving PTH secretion, indicating surgical cure.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Normalisation of serum calcium within the first hour after excision</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Serum calcium takes hours to days to normalise after parathyroidectomy. It is not a reliable real-time intraoperative measure of cure.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>A negative sestamibi scan performed at the end of the procedure</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Sestamibi scanning is a preoperative localisation tool used to identify the adenoma before surgery. It is not repeated intraoperatively to confirm cure.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "The key is intraoperative PTH monitoring. Because PTH has a very short half-life, a fall of more than 50% after excision of the adenoma confirms that no other autonomous parathyroid tissue remains."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Well done. Intraoperative PTH monitoring is the standard method for confirming cure during focused parathyroidectomy — a fall of more than 50% after excision indicates all autonomous tissue has been removed."
                }
              ],
